# Airtable to Discord Webhook Connector

This connector receives an Airtable "new record" webhook payload, fetches the full record data from the Airtable REST API, and sends a formatted message to a Discord channel via an incoming webhook.

## What it does

1. Accepts the webhook payload delivered by Airtable when a new record is created (or via polling the `/payloads` endpoint).
2. For each record ID in the payload, calls the Airtable API to retrieve the complete record (including all field values and the creation timestamp).
3. Constructs a Discord webhook message containing:
   - `content`: the Airtable record ID.
   - `embeds`: a single embed with title "New Record Created", a description, and fields for every Airtable field (plus Record ID and Created Time).
   - `timestamp`: the record's `createdTime` (ISO 8601).
4. POSTs the message to the configured Discord webhook URL.
5. Returns the Discord message IDs for all successfully sent messages.

## Configuration

The connector requires the following configuration values (typically supplied via environment variables):

| Config property | Environment variable | Description |
|-----------------|----------------------|-------------|
| `airtablePersonalAccessToken` | `AIRTABLE_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` | Airtable personal access token with permission to read the base/table. |
| `discordWebhookUrl` | `DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL` | Full Discord incoming webhook URL (e.g. `https://discord.com/api/webhooks/{webhook.id}/{webhook.token}`). |
| `fetch` | (optional) | Custom `fetch` implementation (defaults to global `fetch`). Used for testing. |

## Usage example

```typescript
import { handle, ConnectorConfig, AirtableWebhookPayload } from './connector';

const config: ConnectorConfig = {
  airtablePersonalAccessToken: process.env.AIRTABLE_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN!,
  discordWebhookUrl: process.env.DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL!,
};

// In your webhook handler (e.g. Express, Fastify, etc.):
app.post('/airtable-webhook', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const payload = req.body as AirtableWebhookPayload;
    const result = await handle(payload, config);
    res.json({ success: true, messageIds: result.messageIds });
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
    res.status(500).json({ success: false, error: String(err) });
  }
});
```

## Assumptions

- The Airtable webhook payload follows the structure documented at https://community.airtable.com/other-questions-13/get-new-record-data-with-webhooks-18306 (i.e., it contains a `payloads` array with `changedTablesById` pointing to record IDs).
- The connector must call the Airtable REST API (`GET /v0/{baseId}/{tableId}/{recordId}`) to obtain the `fields` object and `createdTime` for each record.
- The Discord webhook URL already includes the webhook token in the path; no additional authentication headers are required.
- The Discord webhook accepts a JSON payload with at least `content` or `embeds` (we provide both).
- Embed field limits (25 fields per embed, 6000 total characters) are respected by truncating after 25 fields.
- The connector does not implement pagination or deduplication; it processes every record ID present in the incoming payload.
- Errors from either Airtable or Discord (non-2xx responses) are thrown as descriptive `Error` objects including the status code and endpoint.

